\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Yu-Jie Chen, an assistant professor at Taiwan\u8217 s Academia Sinica, said she had not heard of a Chinese secret jail in Dubai, and such a facility in another country would be unusual. However, she also noted that it would be in keeping with China\u8217 s attempts to do all it can to bring select citizens back, both through official means such as signing extradition treaties and unofficial means such as revoking visas or putting pressure on family back home.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 \u8220 (China) really wasn\u8217 t interested in reaching out until recent years,\u8221  said Chen, who has tracked China\u8217 s international legal actions. \u8220 This trend is increasingly robust.\u8221 }{
